THEORY
FTK Imager is a digital forensic software tool developed by AccessData. It is widely used in
computer forensics to acquire and analyze digital evidence from various sources, such as hard
drives, removable media, and forensic images.
FTK Imager provides a range of features and capabilities that assist forensic examiners in their
investigations. Some of its key features include:
1. Disk Imaging: FTK Imager allows forensic professionals to create forensic images or exact copies
of digital storage media. These images can be analyzed without altering or tampering with the
original evidence.
2. File Analysis: The software enables examiners to view and analyze the files and data within a
forensic image. It provides various tools for searching, sorting, filtering, and examining file
metadata and content.
3. Mounting Images: FTK Imager allows forensic images to be mounted as a logical drive, making
it easier to access and analyze the content using standard file explorer interfaces.
4. Keyword Searching: The tool offers advanced keyword searching capabilities, which help
investigators locate specific files, text strings, or metadata within a forensic image or a collection
of images.
5. Data Carving: FTK Imager includes data carving functionality, allowing the recovery of deleted
or hidden files by searching for file signatures and other data patterns.
6. Reporting: The software supports generating comprehensive reports summarizing the findings of
a forensic examination. These reports can be customized and exported in various formats for use
in legal proceedings or documentation.
FTK Imager is commonly used in conjunction with other digital forensic tools and software suites.
It is designed to provide a user-friendly interface while offering powerful capabilities for forensic
analysis and evidence acquisition.
PROCEDURE
I. Take image of the Disk
To perform disk imaging using FTK Imager, follow these general steps:
1. Launch FTK Imager: Start by launching the FTK Imager software on your computer. It
is available for Windows and can be downloaded from the AccessData website.
2. Select the Source: In the FTK Imager main window, click on the "File" menu and choose
the "Create Disk Image" option. A dialog box will appear where you can select the source
device or media you want to image. This can be a physical hard drive, a removable storage
device, or a forensic image file.
3. Choose Destination: After selecting the source, choose the destination where you want to
save the disk image. This should be a separate storage device or location to ensure that the
image does not overwrite or modify the original evidence.
4. Select Imaging Options: FTK Imager offers various imaging options that can be
configured based on your requirements. These options include compression, encryption,
and verification settings. You can choose to compress the image to save disk space, encrypt
it for security, and enable verification to ensure data integrity.
5. Start the Imaging Process: Once you have configured the imaging options, click on the
"Start" button to begin the imaging process. FTK Imager will create a forensic image of
the selected source and save it to the specified destination.
6. Monitor the Progress: During the imaging process, FTK Imager will display the progress
and provide information about the current status, including the number of sectors processed
and the estimated time remaining. You can monitor this information to track the progress
of the imaging process.
7. Verify the Image: After the imaging process is complete, it is crucial to verify the integrity
of the disk image. FTK Imager can perform a verification process to ensure that the created
image is an exact replica of the source device. You should compare the hash values of the
original source and the created image to ensure data integrity.
8. Save a Report: FTK Imager allows you to generate a report summarizing the imaging
process and the details of the created image. You can save this report for documentation
purposes and future reference.
II. Analyse the Image
To analyze a disk image using FTK Imager, you can follow these general steps:
1. 2. 3. 4. Launch FTK Imager: Start by launching FTK Imager on your computer.
Open the Disk Image: In the FTK Imager main window, click on the "File" menu and select the
"Add Evidence Item" option. Browse to the location where the disk image is saved and select it.
FTK Imager will load the disk image for analysis.
Explore the File System: Once the disk image is loaded, you can explore the file system within
the image. FTK Imager displays the directory structure and files contained in the image. You can
navigate through the folders and files to examine their properties and contents.
View File Metadata: FTK Imager provides detailed metadata information for each file in the disk
image. You can select a file and view its metadata, including file name, size, creation/modification
dates, and other relevant information.
5. Search for Files: FTK Imager offers powerful search capabilities to locate specific files or
information within the disk image. You can use keywords, file extensions, or other criteria to
perform searches. The search results will be displayed, allowing you to further analyze the
identified files.
6. Extract Files: If you need to extract specific files from the disk image, you can use FTK Imager
to do so. Select the files you want to extract and choose the "Extract" option from the menu.
Specify the destination folder where you want to save the extracted files.
7. 8. Analyze File Content: FTK Imager allows you to analyze the content of individual files within
the disk image. You can view text-based files, images, documents, and other file types supported
by the software. This can help you extract valuable information and insights from the evidence.
Generate Reports: FTK Imager enables you to generate reports summarizing the analysis of the
disk image. You can customize the report format, include relevant details, and export it for
documentation purposes or legal proceedings.
III. Mount the Image
To mount a disk image using FTK Imager, you can follow these steps:
1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6. 7. 8. Launch FTK Imager: Start by launching FTK Imager on your computer.
Open FTK Imager: In the FTK Imager main window, click on the "File" menu and select the
"Add Evidence Item" option.
Select the Disk Image: In the "Add Evidence Item" dialog box, browse to the location where the
disk image is saved and select it. Click on the "Finish" button to load the disk image into FTK
Imager.
Mount the Disk Image: Once the disk image is loaded, right-click on the disk image entry in the
left-hand pane of FTK Imager. From the context menu, select the "Mount" option.
Choose the Mount Options: A dialog box will appear with mount options. Here, you can specify
the drive letter you want to assign to the mounted disk image. You can also choose whether to
mount the image as read-only or with write access.
Click "OK" to Mount: After configuring the mount options, click on the "OK" button to initiate
the mounting process. FTK Imager will mount the disk image and assign it the specified drive
letter.
Access the Mounted Image: Once the disk image is successfully mounted, you can access it like
any other logical drive on your computer. Open Windows File Explorer, and you will see the
mounted image listed as a separate drive with the assigned drive letter.
Analyze and Work with the Mounted Image: You can now navigate through the mounted disk
image using File Explorer or any other file management tool. You can view and open files, analyze
their content, perform searches, and conduct further analysis on the mounted image.
9. Unmount the Image: When you have completed your analysis or no longer need the mounted
image, it is important to unmount it properly. Right-click on the mounted image in the left-hand
pane of FTK Imager and select the "Unmount" option from the context menu.
